{
  // pfodWeb NOTE: dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion audit, 2026-07-12.
  // Board: DFRobot Beetle ESP32-C3 (SKU DFR0868, coin-sized dev board).
  // Flagged by the discovery sweep for declaring GDI_DISPLAY_FPC_INTERFACE
  // and a block of GDI_* macros (GDI_SPI_SCLK, GDI_SPI_MOSI, GDI_SPI_MISO,
  // GDI_DC, GDI_RES, GDI_CS, GDI_SDCS, GDI_TCS, GDI_SCL, GDI_SDA, GDI_BLK) -
  // all of them #defines pointing at already-declared pin aliases (SCK,
  // MOSI, MISO, A0-A3, SS, SCL, SDA, LED_BUILTIN), not new GPIO
  // declarations. No board.json override existed before this audit (used
  // the chip-level esp32c3/board.json default).
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  //  - https://wiki.dfrobot.com/dfr0868/
  //    Official DFRobot wiki product page (primary vendor source,
  //    authoritative, publishes schematics). Confirmed via WebFetch: the
  //    GDI connector is "a DFRobot dedicated GDI display interface for
  //    connecting a screen using a 18pin-FPC wire" and "comes without a
  //    display attached" - i.e. an optional expansion connector for a
  //    pluggable display module, not a fixed onboard screen. Also
  //    confirmed Onboard LED = GPIO10 and BOOT Button = GPIO9.
  //  - https://wiki.dfrobot.com/SKU_DFR0868_Beetle_ESP32_C3
  //    Same vendor wiki (alternate URL for the same product). Confirmed
  //    via WebFetch: the board's bundled breakout/expansion board "offers
  //    additional power and GPIO pins, battery terminals for the on-board
  //    lithium-ion management system, and a ribbon cable connector for an
  //    optional display" - independently corroborating the GDI connector
  //    is for an optional, user-attached display, not a fixed component.
  //  - https://www.espboards.dev/esp32/dfrobot-beetle-esp32c3/
  //    Independent third-party board database. Confirmed via WebFetch a
  //    BOOT button and separately listed "LED/10" i.e. onboard LED on
  //    GPIO10, and pin-mapping table showing SDA on GPIO8 / SCL on GPIO9 -
  //    matching this board's own pins_arduino.h aliases exactly.
  //  - WebSearch summary of DFRobot/community sources: confirms BOOT
  //    (IO9) and RST buttons for entering download mode / resetting;
  //    battery charging handled by an onboard TP4057 li-ion charge
  //    management chip wired to a solder-pad "BAT" terminal on the
  //    breakout board, not a GPIO-based voltage-sense pin - no BAT/VBAT
  //    alias appears anywhere in this board's pins_arduino.h, so there is
  //    no battery-sense GPIO to tag.
  //  - pins_arduino.h (local, already in repo) - `static const uint8_t
  //    SCL = 9;` duplicates the confirmed GPIO9 BOOT button pin as the I2C
  //    clock alias; used as corroboration alongside the vendor-confirmed
  //    GPIO9 BOOT button finding above.
  //
  // GDI connector (SPI/I2C/A0-A3 repurposed pins): CONFIRMED an OPTIONAL
  // plug-in expansion connector for a separately-purchased display module
  // (18-pin FPC), per two independent fetches of DFRobot's own wiki
  // ("comes without a display attached" / "ribbon cable connector for an
  // optional display"). KEPT general-purpose - SCK/MOSI/MISO/SS/SDA and
  // A0-A3 (GPIO4,6,5,7,8,0,1,2,3) are left at the chip-level default, not
  // excluded, matching the expectation that GDI is a pluggable expansion
  // standard across DFRobot's product line rather than fixed onboard
  // hardware.
  //
  // GPIO9 (opportunistic section 7a button check): CONFIRMED onboard
  // physical BOOT button, per wiki.dfrobot.com and espboards.dev. Standard
  // ESP32-C3 strapping pin (chip-level pinNotes "Must be HIGH for normal
  // boot" retained as-is, not removed - expected/documented for this
  // exact pin, matching the adafruit_qtpy_esp32c3 and
  // waveshare_esp32_c3_zero precedents). Tagged capabilities
  // ["digital_input","button"] only.
  //
  // GPIO10 (LED_BUILTIN): CONFIRMED onboard plain (non-addressable) LED,
  // per wiki.dfrobot.com and espboards.dev ("LED/10"). Not a WS2812/
  // NeoPixel-style addressable LED (no PIN_RGB_LED/RGB_BUILTIN/
  // RGB_BRIGHTNESS macros present in this board's pins_arduino.h, unlike
  // the led_neopixel precedent boards), and GDI_BLK also aliases this same
  // pin as an optional display's backlight-enable line. Left at the
  // chip-level default capabilities (plain digital_output already covers
  // a simple LED) - matching this repo's existing precedent of NOT adding
  // a special capability tag for a plain, non-addressable onboard LED
  // (e.g. lolin_s3_pro's treatment of GPIO14 TFT_LED vs its GPIO38
  // NeoPixel).
  //
  // Alias-conflict check (critical check found earlier this session):
  // this board's own pins_arduino.h declares TX=21, RX=20, SDA=8, SCL=9,
  // SS=7, MOSI=6, MISO=5, SCK=4. GPIO10 (the LED) does not collide with
  // any of these. GPIO9 (the BOOT button), however, DID collide - it was
  // also declared as SCL, which would have let build_boards.js silently
  // append an I2C-clock capability on top of the button-only capability
  // list (that append is independent of anything set here in board.json).
  // Since GPIO9 is a confirmed physical BOOT button pin and not a
  // vendor-confirmed general-purpose I2C header pin, the SCL alias line
  // was deleted outright from pins_arduino.h (not commented out - the
  // parser is comment-blind), and the GDI_SCL macro (which referenced the
  // SCL alias) was repointed at the numeric GPIO value directly so the
  // file stays internally consistent. SDA (GPIO8) was left declared since
  // its pin has no such conflict.
  //
  // See boardsDetails/esp32/esp32c3/dfrobot_beetle_esp32c3/notes.txt for
  // the full research trail.

  "boardName": "ESP32C3",
  "displayName": "ESP32-C3 Dev Module",
  "family": "esp32",
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },
  "dac": {},
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" },
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],
  "connections": ["serial", "ble", "tcp", "http"],
  "pinNotes": {
    "2": "Must be HIGH for normal boot",
    "8": "Must be HIGH or floating on reboot",
    "9": "Must be HIGH for normal boot"
  },
  "chipGpios": {
    "0":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "1":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "2":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "3":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "4":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "5":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input_serial", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "6":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "7":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "8":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "9":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"] },
    "10": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "11": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "20": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "21": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] }
  }
}
